  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This concise, policy-oriented book has begun to circulate beyond its initial publication context: OpenAlex records 4 citations, OAPEN reports 52 downloads, and the book is associated with one teaching-use signal in a University of Li\u00e8ge video. Its strongest footprint is in Belgian scholarship and applied fields, especially around migration, inequality, health, education, and social policy.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Migratie in Belgi\u00eb in 21 vragen en antwoorden has an early but visible impact profile for a scholarly book. The citation record is modest, with 4 OpenAlex citations, but those citations already show reach into different domains rather than a single narrow niche. The citing works include articles and a book chapter published in venues spanning education, ageing, medicine, and criminology, suggesting that the book\u2019s migration overview is being used as a reference point in broader discussions of social conditions and public policy in Belgium.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Usage evidence complements the citation record. OAPEN reports 52 downloads, with activity concentrated in the first two tracked months, indicating that the book was accessed soon after release and continued to attract readers in the short term. The book is also available in open form through multiple repository instances, which likely supports discoverability and reuse. This kind of access matters for a work framed around public understanding and policy relevance.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The surrounding signals point to a clear societal orientation. The book aligns with the SDG theme of Reduced inequalities, and the funding record names the Koning Boudewijnstichting. Together with the subject matter itself, these signals position the book as a resource for evidence-based debate on migration in Belgium. The abstract and the cited contexts both suggest that it addresses questions that are politically and socially salient, rather than remaining within a purely disciplinary audience.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Teaching and public-facing uptake are present as well, though still limited in the record. One YouTube video from Universit\u00e9 de Li\u00e8ge discusses the book, and WorldCat holdings indicate library visibility. Scite also shows one supporting citation statement among 11 total mentions, reinforcing that the book is being used as a source in scholarly writing. Overall, the record indicates a work with early but meaningful uptake, strongest in Belgium and in fields concerned with inequality, migration, and applied social analysis.</p>",
